# Where your Obsidian vault lives. Every stacks command falls back to this when
# --vault is not passed, and `pnpm dev:watch` needs it.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (which is gitignored) and set your own path.
#
# An absolute path is safest — every command resolves it as given, and a
# relative one is read from wherever you happened to run the command. Quote it
# if it contains spaces. Development happens on Windows, so that form is the
# uncommented one; the others are not second-class.
#
#   macOS    STACKS_VAULT=/Users/you/Obsidian/Vault
#   Linux    STACKS_VAULT=/home/you/Obsidian/Vault
#   Windows  STACKS_VAULT=C:\Users\you\Obsidian\Vault
STACKS_VAULT=C:\path\to\your\vault

# Optional, but close to essential if you read recent books.
#
# Open Library is the primary source and is thin on anything published in the
# last year or two. Google Books is the fallback and covers recent titles well —
# but unauthenticated requests share one global quota that is permanently
# exhausted, so without a key it 429s every single time and never answers.
#
# A key is free: console.cloud.google.com -> create a project -> enable the
# "Books API" -> Credentials -> Create credentials -> API key.
# GOOGLE_BOOKS_API_KEY=

# The language you read in, as a two-letter ISO 639-1 code: en, tr, fr, de…
# Defaults to en.
#
# A title search takes its ISBN, publisher, date and cover from one edition in
# this language, so `stacks add "Thinking, Fast and Slow"` records an English
# edition rather than whichever translation a provider happened to list first.
# It changes new lookups only: `enrich` never overwrites a key a note already
# has, so a foreign ISBN, page count or publisher already written stays until
# you edit it.
#
# A value that is not a known two-letter code stops the command rather than
# quietly falling back to English — `english`, `en-GB` and `eng` are all
# refused. Keep any comment on a line of its own: `en # English` is read as
# the whole value and refused too.
# STACKS_LANGUAGE=en

# Port for `pnpm dev:watch`, which runs the site and rebuilds it whenever the
# vault changes. Only worth setting if something else already has 4322.
# PORT=4322

# Set to 1 to let `pnpm dev:watch` listen on your network, not just on this
# machine, so you can open the shelf on a phone without deploying it. It prints
# the address to use. Worth having: two of this project's bugs — the crash and
# the lost WebGL context — only ever appeared on a phone.
#
# Off by default, and worth turning off again afterwards: while it is on,
# anything else on the same Wi-Fi can open the shelf and see what you read. The
# dev build is the same `--public` one the site ships, so `private:` and
# wishlist books stay off it either way.
# STACKS_DEV_HOST=1

# Where the shelf will be served from, e.g. https://stacks.pages.dev.
#
# A static build cannot discover its own origin, and every link-preview scraper
# — Slack, iMessage, WhatsApp, Discord, Twitter — requires an absolute og:image
# URL and silently shows nothing for a relative one. So without this, sharing
# the shelf produces a preview with no picture, which is the one moment the
# generated og.png exists for.
#
# Only needed when deploying. `pnpm dev` has nothing to share, and leaving it
# unset there is correct rather than merely tolerated. `pnpm deploy` refuses to
# run without it — a deploy that silently ships a broken preview is worse than
# one that stops.
#
# On Cloudflare Pages this is https://<your-project>.pages.dev, and you choose
# the project name when you create it, so you know the URL before you first
# deploy. A custom domain goes here instead once you have one.
# SITE_URL=

# Which Cloudflare Pages project `pnpm deploy` uploads to. Defaults to "stacks".
# Only set this if you named the project something else.
# CF_PAGES_PROJECT=stacks

# The Ask the library Worker (docs/spec/ask-the-library.md). Both are read from
# the shell and are never needed to build or test.
#
# The account `pnpm evals:ask` and `pnpm ask:status` talk to. Not a secret, and
# kept out of the repo all the same. The Terraform token is never set here: it
# goes on the terraform command alone, so it cannot reach `pnpm deploy:ask`.
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
#
# Only if a test gateway needs authentication for `pnpm evals:ask` (S12 names it).
# ASK_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
#
# `pnpm ask:status` reads Workers Logs through the telemetry query, which `wrangler login`'s
# scopes cannot (every query answered 403 with it, 2026-11-06). An API token with one
# permission, Account > Workers > Metadata Read-only ("View Workers metadata, settings, and
# observability without access to script source content"), on the account, with an expiry,
# and nothing else; it reads every Worker's logs on the account. ask:status never hands it to wrangler, but wrangler reads this
# file itself on every run (#459).
# ASK_STATUS_TOKEN=

# The Turnstile site key the Ask the library drawer is built with. It is public by design (it sits
# in the page), and it is read when the site is BUILT, so `pnpm deploy:site` must see it: set it
# here or in the shell. The widget (hostname: the production host only) is made by `pnpm turnstile
# --create`, which prints the site key to paste here and sets the secret on the Worker; the secret
# never goes here. The API token that script needs is typed at its prompt and NEVER goes in this file or the
# shell: `pnpm turnstile` refuses to run if `.env` or `.env.local` names it (or any
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN), and so does a shell that holds one.
#
# `pnpm deploy:site` refuses a build with this empty, and a build with one of Cloudflare's test
# keys (1x0000…AA, 2x0000…AB, 3x0000…FF), which any client can pass. Local development uses the
# test key from `packages/site/.env.development.local` instead (git-ignored).
# PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# Optional Plausible analytics (#562, docs/spec/optional-plausible.md, ADR-0138). One variable,
# read when the site is BUILT, from the shell or this file, by `pnpm deploy:site`. It is public by
# design: the site name ends up in the page. Set it to the name of a site that already exists in
# your Plausible account, with the goals `docs/commands.md` lists. The tracker starts only on
# stacks.aymandiab.com, whatever this says. Unset or empty is a complete and supported build: no
# tracker in the bundle, no new CSP source, no request. A value outside lowercase letters, digits,
# dots and hyphens fails the build. The gate builds (`gate:public`, `smoke:render`) strip it.
# PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN=
